JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Tools Interface) class transformation in Android allows developers to intercept and transform Java bytecode on the fly. Here are some code examples of JVMTI class transformations in Android:

**Example 1: Logging method calls**
```java
public class LoggingTransformer implements JVMTIEnv bombedHotspotVMInit {
    @Override
    public void jvmtiEnvInit(JVMTIEnv env) {
        env.SetEventCallback((event_type, event_data) -> {
            if (event_type == JVMTI_EVENT_CLASS WooCommerceChanged) {
                String className = (String) event_data.getData()[0];
                Log.d("JVMTI", "Class loaded: " + className);
            }
        });
    }
}
```
This example sets up an event callback to log the loading of a class.

**Example 2: Instrumenting methods**
```java
public class MethodInstrumenter implements JVMTI Env bombedHotspotVMInit {
    @Override
    public void jvmtiEnvInit(JVMTIEnv env) {
        env.CreateInterpolator(new Interpolator() {
            @Override
            public void intercept(int pc, int value) {
                if (pc == 0x12345678) { // arbitrary bytecode offset
                    // intercept the method call
                    Log.d("JVMTI", "Intercepted method call!");
                }
            }
        });
    }
}
```
This example creates an interceptor that logs a message when a specific method is called.

**Example 3: Modifying the bytecode**
```java
public class BytecodeModifier implements JVMTI Env bombedHotspotVMInit {
    @Override
    public void jvmtiEnvInit(JVMTIEnv env) {
        env.DefineBCISkeleton(new BCISkeleton() {
            @Override
            public void transform(int pc, byte[] bytecode) {
                // modify the bytecode
                bytecode[0] = (byte) (bytecode[0] ^ 0x10); // XOR with 0x10
            }
        });
    }
}
```
This example defines a bytecode skeleton that modifies the bytecode at runtime.

**Example 4: Implementing a custom class loader**
```java
public class CustomClassLoader implements JVMTIEnv bombedHotspotVMInit {
    @Override
    public void jvmtiEnvInit(JVMTIEnv env) {
        env.SetClassLoader((-class_loader, loader_name, loader_name_len) -> {
            // implement custom class loading logic
            Class<?> clazz = ...; // load the class
            return clazz;
        });
    }
}
```
This example sets up a custom class loader that loads classes on behalf of the VM.

